    {
      "id": "3419ce49-c885-4fbf-ad1d-0951c83187b4",
      "name": "Why MCP Is Awesome",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Blog / Why MCP Is Awesome",
      "docTypeId": "215b16e6-766b-4ddc-b2dc-c92eee5363bd",
      "templateId": "2b9d33cc-9a64-4ff1-8918-743de222108e",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP Is Awesome"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-01 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "One protocol, every tool: how the Model Context Protocol turns a pile of bespoke integrations into a single, boring, reliable plug."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "d113dd44-6c38-48bb-8bf4-d2bcc2d22d29",
              "mediaKey": "75c7c9d3-793b-41ea-922c-88beaaf6a7ac",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "56824906-f192-47dc-8cf1-a7d957d85805",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "11fbd409-1ede-4421-9de4-416a62d84a8e",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "473efa32-22d1-41a1-804c-d249835ab84c",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "df123119-82b2-419e-abd0-17796f278dbd",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "371f4a13-744a-40ef-8918-8b610ab75129",
                "key": "56824906-f192-47dc-8cf1-a7d957d85805",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Before MCP, connecting an AI agent to the world meant writing a custom integration for every single tool: one shim for your database, another for your file system, a third for the issue tracker, each speaking its own invented dialect. It was the N×M problem, and it kept everyone writing plumbing instead of features.</p><p>The Model Context Protocol collapses all of that into one contract. A server describes its capabilities once; any agent that speaks MCP can use it immediately. Write the integration once, and every client gets it for free.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "d0de7e94-a06e-4aa9-9a17-8ad89046c4a8",
                "key": "11fbd409-1ede-4421-9de4-416a62d84a8e",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "The integration tangle MCP untangles — one protocol instead of many. Photo: Christopher Gower / Unsplash."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "0294d737-32ca-4949-b73b-79a30dc6fe80",
                        "mediaKey": "beb21f30-e66a-4499-90d1-95f17ec5ebfd",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "371f4a13-744a-40ef-8918-8b610ab75129",
                "key": "473efa32-22d1-41a1-804c-d249835ab84c",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The second thing that makes MCP great is that it is boring in the right places. It does not try to own your model, your UI, or your deployment. It standardises exactly one seam — the one between an agent and its tools — and leaves everything else alone. That narrowness is why it spread: nobody had to rewrite their product to adopt it, only expose a server.</p><p>And because discovery is part of the protocol, an agent can ask a server what it can do and adapt. New capability, zero client-side code. That is the difference between a library and a protocol.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "1ba5a9d9-1658-4f06-bc8c-1f2fd9b40dc9",
                "key": "df123119-82b2-419e-abd0-17796f278dbd",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "The case for MCP, in one sentence"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "Write the tool server once, and every agent on earth can use it tomorrow."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "56824906-f192-47dc-8cf1-a7d957d85805",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "11fbd409-1ede-4421-9de4-416a62d84a8e",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "473efa32-22d1-41a1-804c-d249835ab84c",
