    {
      "id": "a0637d3e-0537-4da5-aceb-c68240532c64",
      "name": "MCP in Practice: Building This Blog",
      "path": "WWW / MCP Blog / MCP in Practice: Building This Blog",
      "docTypeId": "215b16e6-766b-4ddc-b2dc-c92eee5363bd",
      "templateId": "2b9d33cc-9a64-4ff1-8918-743de222108e",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "MCP in Practice: Building This Blog"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-05 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "This entire site — schema, content, media — was modelled and filled in over MCP. Here is what that looked like from the inside."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "6b11ce9a-884b-41c0-ac96-7cee3163aed5",
              "mediaKey": "05d598db-b5d1-4f3d-a42f-2cbfccb4d828",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "6f7cdb47-dd68-48fb-b15c-bd4004b32a32",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "088c851b-06a0-44e4-9848-a4425c02dca2",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "c5187d31-f375-4064-b6e2-d1fd8f30d727",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "4b9cd708-1401-4680-bc3d-7cf6ce1f18d3",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "371f4a13-744a-40ef-8918-8b610ab75129",
                "key": "6f7cdb47-dd68-48fb-b15c-bd4004b32a32",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>This blog is itself a case study. Every document type on this site — the front page, the posts, the three block types you are reading right now — was created over MCP, with no manual clicking in a backoffice and no hand-written database scripts. The schema was modelled in a handful of calls, the media was uploaded the same way, and the words you are reading were placed into blocks one tool call at a time.</p><p>That is the quiet promise of the protocol: if you can describe a capability, an agent can use it. Not just toy capabilities — real ones, like editing a production CMS.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "d0de7e94-a06e-4aa9-9a17-8ad89046c4a8",
                "key": "088c851b-06a0-44e4-9848-a4425c02dca2",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "Every piece had to find its slot — over MCP. Photo: Edge2Edge Media / Unsplash."
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "f9da7571-8eb6-4517-b48c-a47c734d0958",
                        "mediaKey": "200e5262-90f7-46ff-a192-c56f058410f3",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "371f4a13-744a-40ef-8918-8b610ab75129",
                "key": "c5187d31-f375-4064-b6e2-d1fd8f30d727",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The block you are reading is a rich text block inside a block grid property. The grid itself was configured over MCP with a single data type call listing three element types; the paragraphs above were inserted with one call per block. When a call failed, its error message said exactly what to fix — the protocol turns an API surface into a conversation.</p><p>That feedback loop is what separates a demo from a workflow. Schema, content and media were built by the same loop, end to end, on this very project.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "1ba5a9d9-1658-4f06-bc8c-1f2fd9b40dc9",
                "key": "4b9cd708-1401-4680-bc3d-7cf6ce1f18d3",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "This blog's entire build log"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "No clicks, no scripts, no migrations — just protocol calls, and a site that builds itself."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "6f7cdb47-dd68-48fb-b15c-bd4004b32a32",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "088c851b-06a0-44e4-9848-a4425c02dca2",
                "culture": null,
